Create a self-contained prompt that instructs an AI coding agent to write a
Python command-line tool with the following specification: Write a single-file
Python 3 command-line script (with a Termux-style shebang line,
`#!/data/data/com.termux/files/usr/bin/env python`) that removes comments from
Python source files while preserving the file's shebang line and the validity of
the code.
Use `from __future__ import annotations` at the top.
Purpose and behavior: - Accept one or more file or directory paths on the
command line (via argparse) and process every `.py` file found, recursing into
directories. - For each Python file, parse the source with tree-sitter (using
the `tree_sitter_python` grammar and `tree_sitter.Language`/`Parser`) and
collect all `comment` nodes by walking the syntax tree with a tree cursor
(recursive visit over first-child and next-sibling links). - Skip a comment only
if it is the shebang: it sits on line 0 of the file and the file begins with
`#!`.
Every other comment is eligible for removal. - Remove comment text from the
source bytes by byte offsets, working from the last comment to the first so
earlier offsets stay valid.
Handle the case where a comment occupies a full line (remove the whole line
including its newline) versus a trailing comment after code (remove only the
comment text and trailing whitespace before it, keeping the code intact). -
After removal, verify the result is still valid Python using the standard
library `ast` module (`ast.parse`).
If the modified source fails to parse, do not write the file; report it as an
error for that file. - Only write back to disk when the content actually
changed.
Count removed and unremoved (skipped) comments per file. - Represent per-file
results with a `NamedTuple` containing: path, removed count, unremoved count, a
modified flag, and an optional error string.
Concurrency and output: - Process files in parallel using `multiprocessing` (a
process pool), with a worker function that takes a path and returns the result
tuple.
Handle worker exceptions by capturing them into the error field rather than
crashing the run. - Log progress and results with `loguru`: remove the default
handler, add a stderr sink with format `YYYY-MM-DD HH:mm:ss | LEVEL | message`
(colorized tags) at INFO level.
Log per-file summaries and a final total of files modified, comments removed,
and errors. - Print or log errors clearly per file; the script should exit with
a non-zero status if any file failed, and zero otherwise.
Dependencies: `tree-sitter`, `tree-sitter-python`, `loguru`; standard library
otherwise.
Define `PY_LANGUAGE` at module level as `Language(tspython.language())`.
Edge cases to handle: empty files, files with no comments, files with only a
shebang, comments inside strings (must not be treated as comments, which
tree-sitter handles), non-UTF-8 or unreadable files (report error, do not
crash), nonexistent paths (log a warning/error), and files where removing a
comment leaves a blank line that should be dropped only when the comment was on
its own line.
Usage: run as `python script.py [paths...]` with an optional flag to perform a
dry run (report what would change without writing).
Include a `main()` function guarded by `if __name__ == "__main__":`.
Output the complete script as a single file. ---
